($wp$lost-but-found$wp$, $wp$Lost, But Found$wp$, $wp$And for once, I let go. I stopped being the one who had to save everyone.$wp$, $wp$<p>I climbed between the rocks,<br>following a path I did not know,<br>yet somehow,<br>something in me knew where to go.</p>
<p>There was a destination..<br>a temple waiting somewhere above.<br>But somewhere along the way,<br>I stopped walking for the temple<br>and started walking for myself.</p>
<p>The path was never gentle.</p>
<p>There were rocks beneath my feet,<br>mud all overmy shoes,<br>water, dust,<br>hurdles that demanded<br>I slow down.</p>
<p>But I didn&#8217;t.</p>
<p>There were people behind me,<br>people I was supposed to wait for,<br>people I thought needed my hands<br>to pull them through.</p>
<p>And for once,<br>I let go.</p>
<p>I stopped being the one<br>who had to save everyone.<br>I stopped looking behind me.<br>I stopped carrying the weight<br>of people who weren&#8217;t mine to carry.</p>
<p>I walked.</p>
<p>And walked.</p>
<p>And walked.</p>
<p>And somewhere along that climb,<br>I felt strangely distant from myself..<br>as though a quieter part of me<br>had stepped outside my body<br>just to watch.</p>
<p>It watched me climb.</p>
<p>It watched me stumble,<br>catch my breath,<br>place one foot before the other<br>and keep going.</p>
<p>It watched me become<br>someone I hadn&#8217;t seen in a long time.</p>
<p>There was no noise anymore.</p>
<p>No one to save.<br>No one to wait for.<br>No one asking anything of me.</p>
<p>There was only my breath,<br>my feet,<br>the rocks beneath me,<br>and the sky above.</p>
<p>Then came the sunrise..<br>orange spilling into blue,<br>light breaking through<br>everything I had carried<br>for so long.</p>
<p>And there it was.</p>
<p>The temple.</p>
<p>I stood before it,<br>breathless,<br>and for a moment<br>I felt something I couldn&#8217;t name.</p>
<p>Because I had climbed all that way<br>thinking I was looking for a temple.</p>
<p>But perhaps the soul watching me<br>had known all along.</p>
<p>I hadn&#8217;t climbed all that way<br>to find a temple.</p>
<p>I had climbed all that way<br>to find the part of me<br>I had lost somewhere along the way.</p>
<p>And there she was.</p>
<p>She was tired.<br>She was muddy.<br>She was breathless.</p>
<p>But she was free.</p>
<p>And for the first time,<br>I saw myself<br>without needing to fix myself.</p>
<p>I was not someone&#8217;s saviour.<br>I was not someone&#8217;s responsibility.<br>I was not carrying anyone.</p>
<p>I was simply there.</p>
<p>Alive.</p>
<p>Fully.<br>Fiercely.<br>Completely myself.</p>
<p>And perhaps that was what the mountain gave me..</p>
<p>not a destination,<br>not an escape,</p>
<p>but a moment<br>where my soul stepped outside of me<br>long enough to see me<br>finally become myself.</p>$wp$, 'html', $wp$poems$wp$, array[$wp$letting go$wp$, $wp$mountains$wp$]::text[], 'en', $wp$https://apparentlyaditiii.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/gemini_generated_image_1ppnm1ppnm1ppnm1.jpeg$wp$, false, 'published', $wp$2026-10-05T13:50:01+05:00$wp$),
($wp$ek-cutting-chai$wp$, $wp$Ek cutting chai$wp$, $wp$Roz subah, theek 8 baje, tumhe station par dekhti hoon… a stranger worth knowing over tea.$wp$, $wp$<p>Roz subah, theek 8 baje<br> tumhe station par dekhti hoon..<br> haathon mein akhbaar liye,<br> usi kursi par roz baithe hue.<br>Panne nahi ghumate…<br> par aankhein zaroor mila lete ho.</p>
<p>Pata nahi kab<br> ye rail ka safar<br> aankhon se hote hue<br> dil tak pahunch gaya.<br>Tumhari un gehri bhoori aankhon mein<br> bhari woh natkhat si jigyaasa<br> meri jigyaasa ban jaati hai.</p>
<p>Main tumhe aur jaanna chahti hoon.<br>Kahan se aate ho tum?<br> Kahan jaate ho tum?<br> Tumhari aadatein kya hain?<br> Tumhari pasandida baatein kya hain?</p>
<p>Sab jaan-na chahti hoon main…<br> sab jaan-na chahti hoon.</p>
<p>Us bheed-bhari rail mein<br> shayad tum hi ho<br> jo apne se mehsoos hote ho.</p>
<p>Toh…<br> chalo, chalte hain ek shaam.<br>Ek cutting chai ke saath,<br> thodi si baatein karenge..<br> aur shayad,<br> ek doosre ki aankhon mein<br> thoda sa doob bhi jaayenge.</p>$wp$, 'html', $wp$unsent-notes$wp$, array[$wp$hinglish$wp$, $wp$strangers$wp$]::text[], 'en', $wp$https://apparentlyaditiii.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/ab11534e78fb8a1fdaf3754d41715c72.jpg$wp$, false, 'published', $wp$2026-09-23T20:28:54+05:00$wp$),
($wp$would-you-kiss-me-anyway$wp$, $wp$Would You Kiss Me Anyway?$wp$, $wp$The laughter was louder than the TV. By the time we finally fell asleep, I was all…$wp$, $wp$<p>“I am supposed to attach the brackety thing to the side things using a bunch of these little worm guys!”</p>
<p><strong><em>FRIENDS</em></strong> was playing in the background, and it was almost a perfect cosy evening. Our hands were filled with popcorn, our legs tangled into one another, and our conversation had become so magical that we had completely forgotten the world existed outside that little furry white couch.</p>
<p>We spoke for hours.</p>
<p>About everything and nothing. About our lives, our ridiculous stories, the people we loved, the people we didn&#8217;t, and all the little things that somehow felt important when we were together.</p>
<p>The laughter was louder than the TV.<br>By the time we finally fell asleep, I was all curled up to him, all warm and sleepy, with his arm lazily around me and the faint glow of the TV flickering across the room.</p>
<p>It was probably the safest I&#8217;d felt in a long time.</p>
<p>Until around two in the morning, when I woke up with a strange sensation in my gums.<br>It was numb.</p>
<p>Odd.</p>
<p>I ran my tongue over it again, trying to figure out what felt wrong.<br>Sleepily, I got up and walked towards the mirror.<br>And then I smiled.</p>
<p>Or tried to.</p>
<p>I froze.</p>
<p>It was gone.</p>
<p><strong>My tooth.</strong></p>
<p>“Oh my God.”<br>I leaned closer to the mirror.<br>“No. No, no, no…”</p>
<p>It was actually gone.<br>My eyes immediately filled with tears.<br>How could something so tiny suddenly feel like the end of the world?</p>
<p>My smile.<br>My face.<br>My confidence.</p>
<p>Everything I had ever felt pretty about seemed to disappear in those two seconds.<br>And then came the worst thought.</p>
<p><em>He would never kiss me anymore.</em></p>
<p>I heard him wake up behind me.<br>He came rushing towards me, still half asleep, confused by my scream. But I couldn&#8217;t bear to let him see me.</p>
<p>I buried my face in my hands and tried to make him go away.<br>I had suddenly become embarrassed of my own face.</p>
<p>Embarrassed of my smile.<br>Embarrassed of something that, only a few hours ago, I hadn&#8217;t even thought twice about.<br>He didn&#8217;t leave.<br>Instead, he stood there patiently, trying to coax me out of my hands, telling me in that sleepy, soft voice of his that whatever had happened couldn&#8217;t possibly be as terrible as I thought.</p>
<p>And somehow, that only made me cry harder.<br>Eventually, I gathered enough courage to tell him.</p>
<p>My eyes were already overflowing when I finally looked at him and whispered that I had lost my tooth.</p>
<p>I expected him to look surprised.<br>Maybe even a little horrified.<br>Instead, he just looked at me.<br>For a second, neither of us said anything.</p>
<p>Then I broke.</p>
<p>I threw my arms around him and held him so tightly it felt like I could somehow crawl back inside his chest and hide there forever.</p>
<p>He held me just as tightly.</p>
<p>And when he slowly pulled away, he held my face between his warm palms.<br>That was when he finally saw it.<br>The little empty space I had been crying over.<br>The thing I was convinced had made me ugly.</p>
<p>He didn&#8217;t flinch.</p>
<p>Not even for a second.</p>
<p>He simply looked into my eyes, lifted me gently by my thighs, and kissed me.</p>
<p>Just kissed me.</p>
<p>As though there was nothing missing.<br>As though I was still me.<br>As though my smile had never been the reason he loved me in the first place.<br>And when he pulled away, I could feel the tears still sitting on my cheeks.<br>I looked at him, almost unable to believe it.<br>And quietly, somewhere between a laugh and a sob, I whispered to myself—<br><strong>So… he WOULD you kiss me anyway.</strong><br><strong>Yupp, HE WOULD KISS ME ANYWAY.</strong></p>
<p><mark class="accent"><strong>Author’s Note</strong></mark><mark class="accent">&#8211;</mark><br><mark class="accent">The point was never whether you are loved at your best.</mark><br><mark class="accent">The real question is &#8211; </mark><mark class="accent"><strong>will they still love you when one day you wake up and find yourself without everything you thought made you worthy of being loved?</strong></mark><br><mark class="accent">Your self-worth.</mark><br><mark class="accent">Your beauty.</mark><br><mark class="accent">Your money.</mark><br><mark class="accent">Your home.</mark><br><mark class="accent">Your confidence.</mark><br><mark class="accent">Your career.</mark><br><mark class="accent">Your perfectly put-together life.</mark><br><mark class="accent">And maybe, just maybe, your tooth.</mark><br><mark class="accent">We spend so much of our lives trying to become the most lovable version of ourselves. We hide the parts that feel broken, polish the parts that don&#8217;t, and quietly wonder if someone would still choose us if they saw everything we are ashamed of.</mark><br><mark class="accent">What if we stopped asking, “Am I enough for them?”</mark><br><mark class="accent">And started asking, “Will they stay when I don&#8217;t feel like enough for myself?”</mark><br><mark class="accent">Because life is going to take things from us.</mark><br><mark class="accent">There will be days when we don&#8217;t feel beautiful. Days when our bodies change. Days when our plans fall apart, our bank accounts scare us, our careers don&#8217;t work out, our homes don&#8217;t feel like home, and our confidence disappears somewhere between yesterday and today.</mark><br><mark class="accent">There will be versions of us that we don&#8217;t recognize.</mark><br><mark class="accent">And maybe love isn&#8217;t supposed to be reserved for the versions of us that are easiest to love.</mark><br><mark class="accent">Maybe the right kind of love is the kind that looks at the missing tooth, the messy hair, the swollen eyes, the failures, the insecurities, the chaos &#8211; and still sees you.</mark><br><mark class="accent">We are constantly living in fear of being imperfect.</mark><br><mark class="accent">But perhaps the whole point of life was never to become perfect.</mark><br><mark class="accent">Perhaps it was to become imperfectly perfect.. and find someone who knows the difference.</mark><br><mark class="accent">Someone who doesn&#8217;t love you because you have everything.</mark><br><mark class="accent">Someone who loves you even when you don&#8217;t.</mark><br><mark class="accent">Because sometimes, the smallest things we lose teach us the biggest things about what we still have.</mark></p>$wp$, 'html', $wp$unsent-notes$wp$, array[$wp$love$wp$]::text[], 'en', $wp$https://apparentlyaditiii.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/img-20260908-wa0004.jpg$wp$, false, 'published', $wp$2026-09-08T23:08:00+05:00$wp$),
($wp$dead-yet-beautiful-in-full-bloom$wp$, $wp$Dead, Yet Beautiful – In Full Bloom$wp$, $wp$For nothing that withers is ever in vain; it rests, it reshapes, it transforms in disguise.$wp$, $wp$<p class="has-text-align-center">Though wilted. like this flower, you may feel lifeless and dry, <br>But in the nourishing soil of time, you&#8217;ll bloom again. <br>With colours and vibrance you&#8217;ll rise<br>stronger,<br>But even through this trial.. <br>You hold beauty and grace. <br>In your stillness yet catching light. <br>I see you remain a wonder. <br>Dead, yet beautiful. Healing, yet unseen.<br><br>Of petals fallen, do not be penitent,<br>For even in surrender there is art.<br>The earth does not discard what fades..<br>It gathers, softens, turns it into a start.<br>What seems an ending to the hurried eye<br>Is but a quiet becoming beneath.<br>In every fragile, fractured sigh,<br>New roots are weaving strength underneath.<br><br>Though silence wraps around your stem,<br>And shadows linger where light once stayed,<br>There is a pulse beneath the dim,<br>A quiet promise not betrayed.<br>What seems like pause is sacred breath,<br>A gathering of unseen power;<br>For even in the hush of death,<br>You are preparing for your hour.<br><br>And when the rains return unannounced,<br>You will not bloom as once before.<br>But wiser in hue, deeper in stance,<br>Carrying stories within your core.<br>For nothing that withers is ever in vain;<br>It rests, it reshapes, it transforms in disguise.<br>Dead to the season of ache and of pain,<br>Yet beautiful still, in your becoming, you rise.</p>
<p><strong><em>Author’s Note</em></strong><br><br>All the author wants to say is that sometimes what feels like an ending is only a quiet season of becoming. Dead, Yet Beautiful is not about death in the literal sense, but about those moments in life when something within us feels wilted &#8211; hope, joy, certainty, or even identity. The poem speaks to the gentle truth that even in our most fragile states, beauty does not abandon us. It simply rests.<br><br>This piece was born from a small, almost unnoticed moment. I received a bunch of flowers, and among them was one that seemed nearly gone &#8211; dry, its petals falling, its colour fading. Yet I could not look away. Though it was withering, it still held a certain grace. Its beauty had not disappeared; it had only changed. In that flower, I saw something deeply familiar.<br><br>Perhaps I was in a similar state that day.. feeling as though something inside me had quietly died. Yet I chose to get dressed, step outside, and take myself on a small stroll. And somewhere between that wilted flower and the open air, this poem began to write itself. It became a reminder that what feels lifeless today may simply be waiting for nourishment.. for time, for care, for light.<br><br>All the author hopes is that if you ever feel like that fading flower &#8211; dry, tired, unseen.. you remember this: even now, you are still a wonder. And when tended with patience and gentleness, you will bloom again. Not as you were, but as something wiser, deeper, and radiant in a new way.<br><br>Dead, perhaps to a season.<br>But never to your beauty.</p>
<p>🙂</p>$wp$, 'html', $wp$poems$wp$, array[$wp$dead-yet-beautiful$wp$, $wp$renewal$wp$]::text[], 'en', $wp$https://apparentlyaditiii.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/image_editor_output_image1981347664-1772519999325.jpg$wp$, false, 'published', $wp$2026-03-03T11:40:22+05:00$wp$),
($wp$dead-yet-beautiful$wp$, $wp$Dead, yet Beautiful$wp$, $wp$Though wilted like this flower, you may feel lifeless and dry, but in the nourishing soil…$wp$, $wp$<p class="has-text-align-center">Though wilted. like this flower, you may feel lifeless and dry, <br>But in the nourishing soil of time, you&#8217;ll bloom again. <br>With colors and vibrance you&#8217;ll rise<br>stronger,<br>But even through this trial.. <br>You hold beauty and grace. <br>In your stillness yet catching light. <br>I see you remain a wonder. <br>Dead, yet beautiful. Healing, yet unseen.</p>$wp$, 'html', $wp$poems$wp$, array[$wp$dead-yet-beautiful$wp$, $wp$renewal$wp$]::text[], 'en', $wp$https://apparentlyaditiii.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/image_editor_output_image-1806636073-1772517802330.jpg$wp$, false, 'published', $wp$2026-03-03T11:00:15+05:00$wp$),
($wp$i-want-to-become-an-ice-cream-seller$wp$, $wp$I Want to Become an Ice Cream Seller!!$wp$, $wp$We didn't choose dreams based on salaries, stability, or how impressive they sounded when said out loud.$wp$, $wp$<p>As an eight-year-old, when the inevitable question was asked, <em>“What do you want to be when you grow up?”</em> I answered without hesitation: I want to become an Ice Cream Seller.<br>I remember the smile stretching so wide it almost touched my ears. My eyes sparkled with joy, my throat felt thirsty at the mere thought, and my mouth grew cold, as though the ice cream was already there..melting slowly, sweet and inevitable. I savoured the thought quietly, lovingly, until someone laughed and yelled, That’s a weird profession! What will you even earn from selling ice creams?</p>
<p>And honestly, that thought had never crossed my mind.<br>A profession?<br>Earning money?<br>Why even?<br>Wasn’t my parents’ money enough? Didn’t food just appear on the table? Didn’t life simply… happen?</p>
<p>I wailed back, slightly offended, <em>I just want to eat ice cream. All day.</em></p>
<p>Maybe as children, our ambitions were silly. Or maybe they were simply honest.<br>We didn’t choose dreams based on salaries, stability, or how impressive they sounded when said out loud. We chose them based on how they made us <em>feel</em>. Happiness wasn’t a future destination; it was immediate, sensory, and unapologetic. Cold fingers wrapped around a cone. Sticky palms. A racing heart over something as small as an extra scoop.<br>Somewhere along the way, life became serious. Not overnight. But slowly, politely, almost responsibly. We were taught to plan, to prepare, to build a “secure” future. And in doing so, we began postponing joy. Happiness became conditional: after school, after the degree, after the job, after we settle down, after we figure it all out.</p>
<p>We stopped living life the way an eight-year-old does..fully present, unconcerned with outcomes. We started measuring days by productivity instead of pleasure, by progress instead of wonder. Without realizing it, colours faded. Not because life became dull, but because we stopped noticing them.</p>
<p>Maybe growing up isn’t about abandoning childish dreams. Maybe it’s about remembering why we had them in the first place. Not to sell ice cream, necessarily..but to allow ourselves moments of sweetness without guilt. To live, even briefly, without turning every joy into a justification.</p>
<p>I don’t want to eat ice cream all day anymore.<br>But sometimes, I miss the version of me who thought that was enough.</p>
<p>Maybe growing up didn’t take my silly dream away. Maybe, or more precise quietly put it down along with many other small joys, thinking I’d come back for them someday.</p>$wp$, 'html', $wp$reflections$wp$, array[$wp$childhood$wp$, $wp$dreams$wp$]::text[], 'en', $wp$https://apparentlyaditiii.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/c5fa761a9ddc39c662d9d0fc2e0d9069.jpg$wp$, true, 'published', $wp$2026-02-23T16:07:41+05:00$wp$),
($wp$pyaar$wp$, $wp$प्यार$wp$, $wp$Mama, प्यार क्या है? A quiet confession of disillusionment, about love, its sacrifices and its questions.$wp$, $wp$<p class="has-text-align-center">Mama प्यार क्या है ?<br>तुम्हने बोला love is the most beautiful feeling in the world ♡<br>पर तुम्हने बोला क्यों नहीं की प्यार में complications होते है,<br>प्यार में sacrifices होते है,<br>प्यार में दिल टूटता है..<br>और उस टूटे दिल को जोड़ना पड़ता है <br>पर mama इस टूटे दिल के साथ मैं फिर से प्यार कैसे करू<br>अब इस दिल को प्यार करना कैसे सिखाऊ?<br>ये दिल तो बिखर गया है <br>इस दिल को कैसे जोडू?<br>जोड़ने वाला तो चला गया है<br>Mama मैं फिर से प्यार कैसे करू?</p>
<p>This poem is a quiet confession of disillusionment. It reflects the moment when the idea of love shifts from something ideal and promised to something lived and wounded. The poem looks back at love with innocence lost, questioning why its beauty was taught without its cost. There is an underlying exhaustion in the words &#8211; a heart that has loved deeply, broken deeply, and now stands unsure of its own capacity to feel again. The emotions are layered with grief, abandonment, and self-doubt, yet they are not cynical. Instead, the poem reveals of one who is still yearning to believe in love, but now seeks to understand it honestly, with all its fractures. It is the voice of someone learning that love is not just about being held together by another, but about finding the strength to rebuild oneself when that person is gone.</p>$wp$, 'html', $wp$poems$wp$, array[$wp$hindi$wp$, $wp$love$wp$]::text[], 'en', $wp$https://apparentlyaditiii.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/a833c2a251e36188156ff31b538dbb97.jpg$wp$, false, 'published', $wp$2026-01-20T12:36:46+05:00$wp$),
($wp$before-i-move-on$wp$, $wp$Before I Move On$wp$, $wp$2025 was a true roller coaster. The kind that doesn't ask if you're ready before it drops.$wp$, $wp$<p>2025 was a true roller coaster.<br>The kind that doesn’t ask if you’re ready before it drops.<br>I let go of things I once swore were permanent &#8211; people, plans, versions of myself I thought I’d grow old with.<br>And strangely, I held on to things I believed were only passing phases… moments, connections, lessons that quietly stayed and reshaped me.<br><br>There were heartbreaks, real ones.<br>The kind that don’t scream but sit heavy in your chest at night.<br>There were days I felt lost, unsure, and smaller than I’d ever been.<br>But there were also gigantic bursts of laughter, endless storytimes, late-night conversations, and unexpected warmth that made life feel soft again.<br><br>I realized a lot this year.<br>About how attachment can feel like love until it isn’t.<br>About how choosing yourself is lonely at first, but freeing in the long run.<br>About how growth doesn’t always look graceful.. sometimes it looks like walking away, crying in silence, starting over, and still showing up the next day.<br><br>Somehow, through all the chaos, I learned to adore this year.<br><br>Not because it was easy..but because it was honest.<br>It stripped me, tested me, humbled me, and then slowly taught me how to stand on my own without apologizing for it.<br><br>2025 felt like a jalapeño dipped in whipped cream,<br>sharp, confusing, intense, and oddly addictive.<br>Weird, right?<br>Yet once you taste it, you can’t go back.<br>You flinch, you question it, but you go in for another bite! because it wakes something up in you.<br>This year didn’t give me everything I wanted.<br>But it gave me clarity and a version of myself that’s more aware, more rooted, and less afraid of change.<br>And maybe that’s even better. </p>$wp$, 'html', $wp$reflections$wp$, array[$wp$year in review$wp$]::text[], 'en', $wp$https://apparentlyaditiii.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/1767177705826.jpg$wp$, false, 'published', $wp$2025-12-31T15:46:06+05:00$wp$),
($wp$apparently$wp$, $wp$Apparently.$wp$, $wp$Apparently, some thoughts don't fade – they wait. This isn't a blog about having it all figured out.$wp$, $wp$<p>Apparently, some thoughts don’t fade &#8211; they wait.<br>This isn’t a blog about having it all figured out. It’s a place for the in-betweens, the unsent sentences, the versions of me that existed quietly. I’m writing here not to be seen, but to remember.. not for others, but for myself.</p>
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img loading="lazy" width="930" height="1024" src="https://apparentlyaditiii.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/a8d97a4327223426077591a0572801b5465722029463925424.jpg?w=930" alt="" srcset="https://apparentlyaditiii.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/a8d97a4327223426077591a0572801b5465722029463925424.jpg?w=930 930w, https://apparentlyaditiii.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/a8d97a4327223426077591a0572801b5465722029463925424.jpg?w=136 136w, https://apparentlyaditiii.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/a8d97a4327223426077591a0572801b5465722029463925424.jpg?w=272 272w, https://apparentlyaditiii.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/a8d97a4327223426077591a0572801b5465722029463925424.jpg?w=768 768w, https://apparentlyaditiii.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/a8d97a4327223426077591a0572801b5465722029463925424.jpg?w=1440 1440w, https://apparentlyaditiii.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/a8d97a4327223426077591a0572801b5465722029463925424.jpg 1816w" /></figure>$wp$, 'html', $wp$reflections$wp$, array[$wp$start here$wp$]::text[], 'en', $wp$https://apparentlyaditiii.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/a8d97a4327223426077591a0572801b5465722029463925424.jpg$wp$, false, 'published', $wp$2025-12-31T14:29:01+05:00$wp$)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

insert into public.pages (slug, title, kicker, intro, content, content_format) values
  ('about', 'Hello, I’m Aditi.', 'About',
   'A personal archive of thoughts, feelings, and moments that didn''t always ask to be shared.',
   $wp$<p>Apparently, Aditi is a personal archive of thoughts, feelings, and moments that didn’t always ask to be shared, but stayed anyway.<br>This space holds reflections, unsent notes, quiet realizations, and observations made while living, unlearning, and becoming.<br>There’s no promise of answers here, only honesty, growth, and things written as they were felt.</p>$wp$, 'html'),
  ('privacy', 'Privacy', 'Legal', 'Short version: this site doesn’t track you.', $wp$## What’s collected

- No advertising, tracking cookies or third-party analytics scripts run on this site.
- The hosting provider (Vercel) keeps standard server logs (IP address, browser, pages requested) for security and reliability.
- A light/dark theme choice, and which pieces you’ve hearted, are stored in your own browser. They never leave your device.
- Tapping ♥ on a piece adds one to an anonymous counter. Nothing about you is stored with it.

## Newsletter

If you subscribe, your email address is stored in this site’s database (hosted by Supabase) solely to tell you about new pieces. It is never shared or sold. Write to me any time and your address will be deleted.

## Contact form

If you send a message, your name, email and message are stored in the same database so I can read and reply to them. They aren’t shared with anyone, and I’ll delete them on request.

## Questions

Privacy questions: [aditi.sharmaa333@gmail.com](mailto:aditi.sharmaa333@gmail.com).$wp$, 'markdown'),
  ('terms', 'Terms of use', 'Legal', 'Please don’t copy whole pieces without asking.', $wp$## Copyright

All writing and original images on this site are © Aditi unless stated otherwise. You’re welcome to quote a few lines with a link back to the original piece. Reproducing whole pieces, in print or online, needs written permission.

## Sharing

Sharing links to pieces is always welcome.

## Changes

These terms may be updated occasionally. The current version always lives on this page.$wp$, 'markdown'),
  ('disclaimer', 'Disclaimer', 'Legal', 'Personal writing, not advice.', $wp$Everything on this site is personal reflection and creative writing. It describes one person’s experiences and feelings, and isn’t professional, medical, legal or financial advice.

Some pieces are drawn from life and some are imagined; names and details may have been changed. Any resemblance to a specific person is not an invitation to guess who.

Some images are illustrations made with AI tools.$wp$, 'markdown')
